.TH knapsack "" "Command"
.SH NAME
knapsack \- public-key (knapsack) encryption scheme
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBenroll [\fIuser\fB]\fR
.br
\fBxencode [\fIuser\fB]\fR
.br
\fBxdecode\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
enroll -- enroll user in public key cryptosystem
Usage:	enroll
.HE
.B knapsack
is not itself an invokable command; it is the public-key knapsack
encryption scheme, implemented by the routines in
.BR knapsack.c ,
.BR public.c ,
.BR pkio.c ,
and
.BR gpph.c ,
that underlies the
.BR enroll ,
.BR xencode ,
and
.B xdecode
commands.
.PP
A user's public key is derived from a secret passphrase by the
function
.BR knapsack() ,
which seeds a multiprecision linear-congruential random-number
generator from the passphrase text and uses it to build a
superincreasing knapsack vector
\fIk.d[0..63]\fR
together with a pair of modulus/multiplier values
(\fIk.m1\fR/\fIk.w1\fR and \fIk.m2\fR/\fIk.w2\fR)
and a random shuffle order
\fIk.shufl[]\fR.
.B public()
then walks the knapsack through the two modulus/multiplier
transformations, shuffling the terms into
\fIkey[0..63]\fR
by
\fIk.shufl[]\fR,
to produce the public key.
.PP
The public key is stored, one 64-bit component per line in a
16-byte-per-element binary format, in a file named for the user under
.BR /usr/spool/pubkey ,
where anyone may read it to encrypt a message to that user; only the
passphrase holder can regenerate the matching private knapsack, so
only that user can decrypt it.
.IP \fBenroll\fR
Enroll or re-key a user.
Called with no argument, it enrolls (or re-keys) the caller, as found
by
.BR getlogin() ;
called as
.BI "enroll " user
(normally only useful for the superuser), it enrolls the named
.IR user .
If the user is already enrolled,
.B enroll
first asks for the current passphrase and verifies it by recomputing
the public key and comparing every component against the stored file
before asking for a new passphrase (twice, to catch typing mistakes).
Entering an empty new passphrase removes the user's public-key file,
withdrawing the user from the scheme.
.IP \fBxencode\fR
Read standard input and write it, encrypted, to standard output, in
a mail-compatible text format (base-95 digits, one block per line).
Called with no argument, it prompts for a passphrase and builds a
public key from it on the fly; called as
.BI "xencode " user
it reads
.IR user 's
stored public key instead and does not prompt.
.IP \fBxdecode\fR
Prompt for a passphrase, rebuild the matching private knapsack, and
decrypt standard input (in the format written by
.BR xencode )
to standard output.
An empty passphrase exits immediately without reading any input.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 24n
\fB/usr/spool/pubkey/\fIuser\fR	stored public key for \fIuser\fR
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B xmail
.SH NOTES
The directory
.B /usr/spool/pubkey
must be owned by, and mode 0700 for, a dedicated user (conventionally
named
.BR xmail );
the commands
.BR enroll ,
.BR xencode ,
and
.B xdecode
must be installed set-user-ID to that owner so that ordinary users can
read and update their own public-key entries without being able to
tamper with anyone else's.
